<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIDeviceFamily</key>
	<array>
		<!-- iPhone and iPad. The UI is a two-tab Shell; it does not
		     assume a phone-sized screen. -->
		<integer>1</integer>
		<integer>2</integer>
	</array>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>XSAppIconAssets</key>
	<string>Assets.xcassets/appicon.appiconset</string>

	<!--
		The return leg of Google sign-in.

		This is the iOS half of what WebAuthenticatorCallbackActivity does
		on Android: it claims the `link` scheme so the system browser can
		hand `link://auth?...` back to this app. AppDelegate.OpenUrl
		forwards it to WebAuthenticator from there.

		`link://auth` is internal to Link and Fellowship and is never
		registered with Google — Google only ever sees Fellowship's HTTPS
		callback, and would reject a custom scheme on a Web client. See
		README.md, "The four places `link://auth` is written", which this
		file is one of.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.thebleedingdeacons.intergroup.link</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>link</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
		App Transport Security: deliberately left at its defaults, and
		deliberately not pinned.

		There is no NSAppTransportSecurity key anywhere in this file, and that
		is the strict setting rather than a gap — ATS defaults to requiring
		HTTPS with modern TLS and refusing arbitrary loads. Nothing here
		weakens it, and nothing should: the app talks to exactly one host.

		Semgrep reports this file under
		swift.insecure-communication.ats.ats-pinning.ATS-consider-pinning,
		which flags the *absence* of NSPinnedDomains. That finding is
		accepted, on purpose, and is expected to stay open. It is advice, not
		a defect.

		Why not pin. Pinning binds the app to a specific public key for
		aa-bristol.org. The certificate is renewed on the intergroup's own
		hosting on a schedule nobody here controls, and a renewal that changes
		the key would leave every installed copy unable to reach the server —
		silently, and for as long as it took to ship and for members to
		install an update. That is a worse failure than the one pinning
		prevents: the attack it defends against needs a CA to mis-issue for
		this domain, while the outage it risks needs only an ordinary
		certificate rotation.

		What would change the answer: pinning becomes reasonable alongside a
		backup pin for the next key and a release process that rotates the two
		together, which is a real piece of operational work rather than a
		plist entry. Revisit it then, and not by adding NSPinnedDomains here
		on its own.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
